<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Roger Williams</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1603–<a href="/years/1683">1683</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Roger Williams was an English Puritan minister banished from the Massachusetts Bay Colony in 1635 for his views on religious liberty and native land rights. In 1636 he founded Providence on land obtained from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narragansett-people">Narragansett</a>, and he secured a charter for Rhode Island, which separated church and state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/roger-williams.jpg" alt="Roger Williams" width="320" height="652" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roger_Williams_statue_by_Franklin_Simmons.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Roger Williams was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, probably in 1603. His birth records were destroyed in the Great Fire of London in 1666, and he gave contradictory information about his age during his life. His father was a merchant tailor in Smithfield. As an adolescent he apprenticed under the jurist Sir Edward Coke, who paid for his schooling at Charterhouse, and he received a Bachelor of Arts from Pembroke College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, in 1627. He knew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>, Greek, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and French. He took holy orders in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a> but became a Puritan at Cambridge, then a Separatist, and he and his wife Mary Bernard sailed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bristol">Bristol</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> in December 1630.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> anchored at Nantasket on 5 February 1631. Williams declined a post at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> because it was 'an unseparated church', and he argued that civil magistrates must not punish breaches of the first table of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ten-commandments">Ten Commandments</a> and that individuals should be free to follow their convictions in religion. He lived at Plymouth Colony and at Salem, and in December 1632 he wrote a tract condemning the king's charters and questioning the right to the land without purchase from the Native Americans. In October 1635 the General Court of Massachusetts Bay convicted him of sedition and heresy and ordered him banished.</p><p>When the sheriff came in January 1636, Williams had slipped away three days earlier during a blizzard. He reached a Wampanoag winter camp at Raynham, where the sachem <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massasoit">Massasoit</a> sheltered him for three months. In spring 1636 he and four companions founded Providence on land acquired from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narragansett-people">Narragansett</a> sachems Canonicus and Miantonomi. A majority of heads of households governed it, only in civil matters. In 1638 Williams and about twelve others formed a congregation now recognised as the First Baptist Church in America. In July 1644 he obtained a charter from Parliament, and in 1647 Providence, Portsmouth, Newport and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warwick-rhode-island">Warwick</a> united as the Colony of Rhode Island and Providence Plantations.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>In 1643 Williams published in London A Key into the Language of America, the first book-length study of a Native American language in English. His The Bloudy Tenent of Persecution for Cause of Conscience, published in 1644, caused a great uproar: at least 60 pamphlets addressed it between 1644 and 1649, and Parliament ordered the public hangman to burn all copies. Williams held that citizenship and religion should be separate, and Rhode Island is described as the first government in the Western world to guarantee religious freedom in its founding charter. He later became a 'seeker' who belonged to no specific church, and he mediated for nearly 40 years between the Native Americans and the colony.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Roger Williams” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Roger Williams</b> set up Providence Plantations in 1636 with a formal separation of church and state.</blockquote><a href="/movement/puritans">Puritans · People</a></li><li><blockquote>Significant contributions to the principles of modern secularism came from theologians and Christian writers such as St. Augustine, William of Ockham, Marsilius of Padua, Martin Luther, <b>Roger Williams</b>, John Locke and Talleyrand.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/secularism">Secularism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Banished from the Massachusetts Bay Colony, <b>Roger Williams</b> founded Providence in 1636 on land obtained from the Narragansett.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-rhode-island-and-providence-plantations">Colony of Rhode Island and Providence Plantations</a></li><li><blockquote>Dissenters were expelled: <b>Roger Williams</b> and Anne Hutchinson went to what became the Colony of Rhode Island and Providence Plantations.</blockquote><a href="/state/massachusetts-bay-colony">Massachusetts Bay Colony</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/providence-rhode-island">Providence</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1655" aria-label="London on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4547 381 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Roger Williams"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4997 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4997 651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1655">What was here in 1655? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Boast not proud English, of thy birth and blood Thy Brother Indian is by birth as Good. Of one blood God made Him, and Thee, and All. As wise, as faire, as strong, as personall.&#8221;</p><footer>A Key into the Language of America (1643), chapter 1 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/63701/pg63701-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;An enforced uniformity of religion throughout a nation or civil state, confounds the civil and religious, denies the principles of Christianity and civility, and that Jesus Christ is come in the flesh.&#8221;</p><footer>The Bloudy Tenent of Persecution (1644), preface &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/65739/pg65739-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It is the will and command of God that, since the coming of his Son the Lord Jesus, a permission of the most Paganish, Jewish, Turkish, or anti-christian consciences and worships be granted to all men in all nations and countries…&#8221;</p><footer>The Bloudy Tenent of Persecution for Cause of Conscience (1644), preface &#183; original &#183; in the modernised spelling of the 1848 Hanserd Knollys Society edition &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/65739/pg65739-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;God requireth not an uniformity of religion to be enacted and enforced in any civil state; which enforced uniformity, sooner or later, is the greatest occasion of civil war, ravishing of conscience…&#8221;</p><footer>The Bloudy Tenent of Persecution for Cause of Conscience (1644), preface &#183; original &#183; in the modernised spelling of the 1848 Hanserd Knollys Society edition &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/65739/pg65739-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…when they have opened a gap in the hedge, or wall of separation, between the garden of the church and the wilderness of the world, God hath ever broke down the wall itself, removed the candlestick, &amp;c.&#8221;</p><footer>Mr. Cotton's Letter Examined and Answered (1644) &#183; original &#183; in the modernised spelling of the 1848 Hanserd Knollys Society edition &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/65739/pg65739-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
